 Part I, Empirical wave measurements and validation of a non-stationary wave prediction model

Title: A coastal report about waves around a stone pier: Part I, Empirical wave measurements and validation of a non-stationary wave prediction model
Abstract: In this report, both empirical measurements and numerical simulations are conducted to investigate the wave field around a stone pier. Wave currents and wave periods are measured in the offshore region, near the pier and at various points along the shore. A non-stationary wave prediction model is developed to simulate the wave field around the pier, using the measured wave statistics as input. The model is validated by comparing the simulated wave patterns with the measured data. The results show that the model accurately captures the wave field characteristics, such as wave direction, period and amplitude, at the measurement points. The non-stationary wave prediction model can therefore be used as a useful tool for predicting wave conditions around a stone pier, which is essential for designing and maintaining coastal structures